load ../revision-rule-correspondence.maude

--- Coverage: a unique match records the correspondence in the activation.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init1 =>* { active(e1) corr(e1, work, renamed) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I1 BITE: the renamed rule admits NO new firing for the trigger the old
--- name already fired on. This is the executed defect -- two timer.wait
--- effects -- stated as an unreachability.
search in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init1 =>* { fired(e1, renamed, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- THE SAFETY PROPERTY of the ruled design, and the reason it was ruled: with
--- the SAME unique match but no operator confirmation, the renamed rule FIRES.
--- A derived match suppresses nothing on its own, so the delete-plus-copy case
--- -- one removed rule, one added rule whose body was copied from it, a unique
--- hash match -- cannot silently swallow a firing that should happen. The
--- failure direction is a duplicate, which the operator can see.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init1unconfirmed =>* { fired(e1, renamed, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- The evidence is still recorded when unconfirmed: the operator has to be able
--- to see what was proposed and that nobody confirmed it.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init1unconfirmed =>* { corr(e1, work, renamed) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I1 NON-VACUITY: the same revision with no prior firing DOES fire under the
--- new name. Without this, the No-solution above would be satisfied by a state
--- that is unreachable for unrelated reasons rather than by the carry.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init1bare =>* { fired(e1, renamed, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I2: under an ambiguous match no correspondence is recorded, so the
--- colliding NEW rule still fires. This search is the uniqueness guard's bite:
--- admitting a correspondence on an ambiguous match pairs work with extra and
--- suppresses this firing, turning a duplicate into a MISSING emission, and
--- this Solution becomes a No solution.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init2 =>* { fired(e1, extra, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I2, the other side: with nothing recorded, the renamed rule fires too. The
--- guard's failure direction is a duplicate -- today's behaviour -- which is
--- the safe one.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init2 =>* { fired(e1, renamed, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I3 CORRECTED, and it is a bite rather than coverage. A body change under the
--- SAME rule name admits no new firing: the name-keyed carry suppresses it. The
--- retracted version of this record claimed the opposite and hid it by naming
--- the changed rule differently, which is a rename with no match.
search in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init3same =>* { fired(e1, work, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- Middle of the ladder: a firing recorded at e1 and renamed at the e1->e2
--- activation is carried too. Without this fixture carryTo(e1, N, e2, C) is
--- dead code and a regression in it is free.
search in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  initMid =>* { fired(e2, w3, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- The recorded correspondence is a function only because at most one is ever
--- recorded per activation. Documented as a reachability fact, not proved as a
--- design invariant: `step` is ill-defined on a state binding one name twice,
--- and Decision 2's uniqueness requirement is what is supposed to exclude it.
search in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init2 =>* { corr(e1, work, renamed) corr(e1, work, extra) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- Coverage: two activations, two recorded correspondences.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init4 =>* { active(e2) corr(e1, work, renamed) corr(e2, renamed, w3) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .

--- I4 BITE: a rename carried across TWO revisions still suppresses. The chain
--- composes; it is not one hop.
search in WHIPPLESCRIPT-REVISION-RULE-CORRESPONDENCE :
  init4 =>* { fired(e2, w3, t1) RESIDUAL:Cfg } .
